<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> The existentialist claim that "existence precedes essence" means:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Human beings exist before they are born</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Human beings have no predetermined nature or purpose; they exist first and create what they are through their choices</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Physical existence is more real than abstract essences</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Moral duties exist independently of human beings who follow them</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For existentialists there is no human nature fixed in advance. We exist first and then make ourselves what we are through our choices.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Existentialism denies that human beings come with a predetermined nature or purpose. Ask what comes first and what is made afterward.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> When de Beauvoir uses the term "ambiguity" to describe the human condition, she means:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">That moral questions have no clear answers</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">That language is inherently vague</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">That human beings are simultaneously free consciousnesses that transcend their situation and situated beings who are shaped by conditions they did not choose</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">That the boundary between good and evil is always unclear</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For de Beauvoir we are ambiguous because we are both: free consciousness that transcends any situation, and embodied, situated beings shaped by conditions we did not choose.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">De Beauvoir's ambiguity is not vagueness or moral uncertainty. Ask what two sides of the human condition she holds together.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> De Beauvoir argues that freedom cannot be escaped. By this she means:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">That social structures always constrain human behavior</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Even the refusal to choose, or the decision to drift passively, is itself a choice; there is no neutral position outside freedom</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">That human beings are determined by their circumstances and cannot act otherwise</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">That political freedom, once achieved, cannot be taken away</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. There is no neutral position outside freedom. Refusing to choose, or drifting, is itself a choice we are responsible for.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">De Beauvoir does not mean that freedom is guaranteed or that we are determined. Ask what she says about refusing to choose.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> De Beauvoir's concept of "bad faith" refers to:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Dishonesty in professional dealings</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">The failure to honor religious commitments</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">The various ways in which human beings deny or flee from their own freedom, acting as though their situation determines their responses without remainder</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Arguing in ways designed to mislead rather than illuminate</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Bad faith is the flight from freedom: pretending that our situation, role, or nature dictates what we do, so that we are not responsible for it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">In this tradition bad faith is not ordinary dishonesty. It is a kind of self-deception about something specific. Ask what is being denied.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> De Beauvoir argues that the ethical task is to "assume" one's freedom. She means:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">To take political power in one's community</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">To presuppose that freedom exists before proving it</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">To honestly acknowledge and accept the full weight of one's freedom rather than fleeing it into bad faith</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">To assert one's freedom against those who would deny it</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. To assume freedom is to take it up honestly, owning the weight and responsibility of one's choices instead of fleeing into bad faith.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">'Assume' here means taking something up and owning it, not presupposing it. Ask what de Beauvoir thinks the ethical person does with their freedom.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> De Beauvoir's "sub-man" is characterized by:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Total commitment to a political or institutional cause</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Passive drift and the refusal to engage, making the person available to be recruited by whoever offers a cause</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Relentless pursuit of adventure and novelty</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Cynical dismissal of all values following a failed absolute commitment</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The sub-man flees freedom through apathy. He refuses to engage with anything, which leaves him available to be swept up by whatever movement offers him a role.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Each inauthentic attitude evades freedom in its own way. Ask which one does so by passivity and refusal to engage.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> De Beauvoir argues that the "serious man" represents a form of bad faith because:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">He refuses to commit to anything and therefore avoids responsibility</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">He is too intellectually rigorous to form genuine human relationships</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">He subordinates his freedom entirely to an external absolute (the Cause, the Party, the Nation), offloading responsibility onto the object of his commitment and acting as though it, not he, is responsible for his choices</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">He takes morality too seriously, making him inflexible when circumstances require compromise</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The serious man makes some external absolute, a Cause, Party, or Nation, the source of all value and loses himself in it. He treats the cause, not himself, as responsible for what he does.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The serious man's problem is not lack of commitment. Ask what he does with his freedom when he gives himself over to a cause.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> De Beauvoir's "nihilist" attitude emerges from:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">A genuine philosophical conclusion that nothing has value</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Disappointed seriousness: the serious man who discovers that his absolute is empty, and turns the resulting disillusionment into a pose of universal indifference</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">A principled rejection of all moral authority in favor of personal freedom</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">The natural response of any rational person who has read Nietzsche carefully</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The nihilist is a disappointed serious man. When his absolute collapses, he concludes that nothing has value and turns disillusionment into a pose of indifference.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">De Beauvoir traces nihilism to a specific experience, not a philosophical argument. Ask which earlier attitude it grows out of.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> What do all five inauthentic attitudes (sub-man, serious man, nihilist, adventurer, passionate man) have in common, according to de Beauvoir?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">They all involve excessive concern with the opinions of others</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">They all reject the possibility of political engagement</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Each one represents a different way of evading the full acknowledgment of freedom: either denying it, dissolving it into an object, or exercising it without genuine engagement with its implications</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">They all result from a failure to read serious philosophy</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. All five attitudes evade the full acknowledgment of freedom. Some deny it, some dissolve it into an object, and some exercise it without engaging what it is for.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Look for what unites the sub-man, the serious man, the nihilist, the adventurer, and the passionate man. Ask what each one fails to face.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> The "adventurer" attitude, in de Beauvoir's typology, fails because:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The adventurer is too committed to a single cause</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The adventurer pursues the exercise of freedom as an end in itself, without caring about the content of what freedom is for, making his freedom ultimately self-enclosed and shallow</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Adventure is too dangerous to qualify as an ethical life</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The adventurer refuses to engage with political questions</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The adventurer loves action and conquest for their own sake but is indifferent to what they serve. His freedom never reaches out to the freedom of others, so it stays shallow and self-enclosed.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The adventurer is not over-committed. If anything, the reverse. Ask what is missing from a freedom pursued only for its own sake.</div>
  </div>
</div>

